  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)]^{\u2032}}{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x)}{2x-2} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x)}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x)}{x-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x)]^{\u2032}}{[x-1]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)}{1} \\\\  & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi  \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
